# ======================= 1.1 SHOR =======================
A['1.1']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.11–13 (3.6 Shor's algorithm: problem, steps, example); QAI.pdf p.29–31 (QFT, phase estimation, order-finding algorithm), p.32 (factoring & cryptography)",
note="Your textbook's worked example (N = 91, a = 10, QAI-ii.pdf p.12–13) has an arithmetic slip: 10⁵ mod 91 = 82, not 1 (the true period is 6). This answer uses the verified example N = 15.",blocks=[
("def","**Shor's algorithm** (Peter Shor, 1994) is a quantum algorithm that **factors a large composite integer N in polynomial time**. It converts factoring into **period (order) finding** – finding the smallest r with aʳ ≡ 1 (mod N) – and solves that step on a quantum computer using **superposition, modular exponentiation and the Quantum Fourier Transform (QFT)**."),
("h","1. Why it is needed"),
("b",["The best classical algorithm (General Number Field Sieve) needs **super-polynomial (near-exponential) time** in the number of digits of N.","Shor's algorithm needs only about **O((log N)³)** operations → an **exponential speed-up**.","Only the period-finding step is quantum; the rest is classical number theory."]),
("h","2. Structure of the algorithm"),
("t",["Part","Work done","Type"],[["Step 1","Choose random a (1 < a < N); if gcd(a, N) ≠ 1 a factor is already found","Classical"],["Step 2","Find the period r of f(x) = aˣ mod N","**Quantum** (QFT)"],["Step 3","If r is even and a^(r/2) ≢ −1 (mod N): factors = gcd(a^(r/2) ± 1, N)","Classical"],["Step 4","Otherwise repeat with a new a","Classical"]],[0.9,4.4,1.3]),
("h","3. Quantum part – period finding (detailed)"),
("n",["**Registers:** first (input) register of t qubits with Q = 2ᵗ, N² ≤ Q < 2N², in |0⟩; second (output) register of n = ⌈log₂N⌉ qubits in |1⟩.",
"**Superposition:** Hadamard on every input qubit → |ψ₁⟩ = (1/√Q) Σₓ |x⟩|1⟩ (all x from 0 to Q−1 at once).",
"**Modular exponentiation U_f:** |x⟩|y⟩ → |x⟩|y·aˣ mod N⟩ gives |ψ₂⟩ = (1/√Q) Σₓ |x⟩|aˣ mod N⟩ – f is computed for every x in one run (**quantum parallelism**).",
"**Measure the second register** (optional, simplifies analysis): we get some value f(x₀); the first register collapses to the periodic state (1/√M) Σⱼ |x₀ + j·r⟩.",
"**Inverse QFT** on the first register: the periodic pattern interferes so amplitude concentrates on y ≈ k·Q/r (k = 0, 1, …, r−1).",
"**Measure** the first register to get y; compute y/Q ≈ k/r and use the **continued-fractions algorithm** to obtain r. Check aʳ mod N = 1; repeat a few times if needed (QAI.pdf p.31)."]),
("c",["|0⟩ᵗ","|1⟩ⁿ"],[("H",0,HT,0),("U2",0,1,"U_f\naˣ mod N",1),("M",1,2),("QFT",0,"QFT⁻¹",3),("M",0,4)],"Quantum circuit for the period-finding part of Shor's algorithm",["y ≈ kQ/r","f(x₀)"]),
("p","**Components:** H⊗t creates all inputs x; U_f (modular exponentiation, a controlled-U^(2ʲ) cascade) writes aˣ mod N into the second register and entangles the registers; measuring the second register leaves a periodic first register; QFT⁻¹ converts period into peaks; final measurement samples a peak."),
("h","4. Formulas"),
("f",["QFT: |x⟩ → (1/√Q) Σ_y e^(2πixy/Q) |y⟩   (QAI.pdf p.29)","Period: aʳ ≡ 1 (mod N)  ⇒  (a^(r/2) − 1)(a^(r/2) + 1) ≡ 0 (mod N)","Factors:  p = gcd(a^(r/2) − 1, N),  q = gcd(a^(r/2) + 1, N)"]),
("p","Symbols: N = number to factor, a = random base, r = period/order, Q = 2ᵗ size of input register, y = measured value, gcd = greatest common divisor."),
("h","5. Worked example: N = 15"),
("n",["Choose a = 7; gcd(7, 15) = 1 ✔.","f(x) = 7ˣ mod 15 = 1, 7, 4, 13, 1, 7, 4, 13, … (period r = 4).","Quantum part with t = 8 (Q = 256 ≥ 15² = 225): measurement gives y ∈ {0, 64, 128, 192} with probability ¼ each. Suppose y = 192 → y/Q = 192/256 = 3/4 → denominator **r = 4**.","r is even and 7² = 49 ≡ 4 (mod 15), not ≡ −1 ✔.","p = gcd(49 − 1, 15) = gcd(48, 15) = **3**;  q = gcd(49 + 1, 15) = gcd(50, 15) = **5**  →  **15 = 3 × 5**."]),
("h","6. Significance in breaking classical cryptosystems"),
("b",["**RSA** security depends on the difficulty of factoring N = p·q; **Diffie–Hellman and ECC** depend on discrete logarithms – Shor's method solves both (they are period-finding problems).","A large fault-tolerant quantum computer would break today's public-key infrastructure (HTTPS, digital signatures, banking).","Response: **post-quantum cryptography** (lattice-, code-, hash-based schemes) and quantum key distribution (QAI-ii.pdf p.13)."]),
("h","7. Limitations"),
("b",["Needs about 2n + 3 logical (error-corrected) qubits and a deep circuit → millions of physical qubits for 2048-bit RSA; so far only small numbers (15, 21) have been factored on real devices.","Probabilistic: some choices of a fail (r odd or a^(r/2) ≡ −1); success probability ≥ ½ per attempt."]),
("concl","Shor's algorithm = classical reduction + quantum period finding with the QFT; it factors integers in polynomial time and therefore threatens RSA-type cryptography."),
("tip","**“A-R-G”: pick a → find r (quantum) → take gcd.** Common mistakes: forgetting gcd(a, N) = 1, forgetting the condition ‘r even and a^(r/2) ≠ −1’, and calling the whole algorithm quantum (only period finding is)."),
])
# ======================= 1.2 GROVER =======================
A['1.2']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.13–15 (Grover's algorithm: classical search problem, quantum search, Fig. 3.9, working, 8-element example, D = 2|s⟩⟨s| − I), p.17–18 (question bank)",blocks=[
("def","**Grover's algorithm** (Lov Grover, 1996) searches an **unsorted (unstructured) database of N items** for a marked item using about **√N** oracle queries, instead of the N/2 average (N worst case) needed classically. It works by **quantum amplitude amplification** and gives a **quadratic speed-up**."),
("h","1. The search problem"),
("b",["Items are indexed x = 0 … N−1 (N = 2ⁿ, so n qubits). An **oracle** function f(x) = 1 only for the target w, else 0.","Classical: no structure to exploit → check items one by one → **O(N)**.","Quantum: **O(√N)** queries – proved optimal for unstructured search."]),
("h","2. Steps of the algorithm (QAI-ii.pdf p.14)"),
("n",["**Initialise:** n qubits in |0…0⟩; apply Hadamard to all → equal superposition |s⟩ = (1/√N) Σₓ |x⟩ (every item has amplitude 1/√N).",
"**Oracle U_f (phase flip):** U_f|x⟩ = (−1)^f(x)|x⟩ – the sign of the target amplitude becomes negative (implemented with an ancilla in |−⟩ via phase kick-back).",
"**Diffusion operator D = 2|s⟩⟨s| − I:** ‘inversion about the mean’ – each amplitude a is replaced by 2·mean − a, so the negative (marked) amplitude grows and the others shrink.",
"**Repeat** the Grover iteration G = D·U_f about k ≈ (π/4)√N times.",
"**Measure** all qubits → the target w is obtained with high probability."]),
("c",["|0⟩ⁿ"],[("H",0,HN,0),("U2",0,0,"U_f",1),("U2",0,0,"D",2),("BAR","repeat ≈ (π/4)√N times",3),("M",0,3)],"Grover's circuit (after Fig. 3.9, QAI-ii.pdf p.14): state preparation, oracle, diffusion, measurement",[""]),
("p","**Components:** H⊗n = state preparation (all N items at once); **oracle** = marks the answer by a minus sign (does not reveal it); **diffusion** = converts the hidden sign into larger amplitude; **measurement** = reads the amplified item."),
("h","3. Worked example: N = 8, target |6⟩ (book example)"),
("t",["Stage","Amplitude of each of the 7 other items","Amplitude of |6⟩","P(measure 6)"],[["After H (|s⟩)","0.354","0.354","12.5 %"],["After oracle","0.354","−0.354","12.5 %"],["After diffusion (iteration 1)","0.177","0.884","78.1 %"],["After iteration 2","−0.088","0.972","**94.5 %**"]],[2.2,2.1,1.2,1.1]),
("p","Calculation of iteration 1: mean = (7 × 0.354 − 0.354)/8 = 0.265. Others: 2(0.265) − 0.354 = 0.177. Target: 2(0.265) + 0.354 = 0.884. Iterations needed ≈ (π/4)√8 ≈ 2. Classically about 4 checks on average."),
("h","4. Searching an unsorted database (2025 question)"),
("b",["**Encode** each record index as a basis state; a database of 1 million entries needs only 20 qubits.","The **oracle** compares the record at index x with the search key (e.g. a phone number) and flips the phase if it matches.","Grover needs ≈ (π/4)√10⁶ ≈ **785** queries instead of ≈ 500 000 on average classically."]),
("h","5. Formulas"),
("f",["|s⟩ = (1/√N) Σₓ |x⟩ ;  U_f = I − 2|w⟩⟨w| ;  D = 2|s⟩⟨s| − I","k_opt ≈ (π/4)√(N/M)   (M = number of marked items)","P(success after k iterations) = sin²((2k + 1)θ),  where sin θ = √(M/N)"]),
("h","6. Applications and limitations"),
("b",["Applications: database search, **SAT / NP-complete** problem solving, minimum finding, collision finding, **cryptanalysis** (brute-force key search: halves the effective key length, so AES-128 → ~64-bit security), nearest-neighbour search in QML.","Limitations: only quadratic (not exponential) speed-up; the oracle must be built as a quantum circuit; data must be loaded (QRAM); too many iterations **over-rotate** and reduce the success probability."]),
("concl","Grover's algorithm finds a marked item among N unsorted items in O(√N) steps by repeating oracle + inversion about the mean – a provably optimal quadratic speed-up."),
("tip","**“Superpose – Mark – Flip about mean – Repeat √N – Measure.”** Never write ‘exponential speed-up’ for Grover."),
])
# ======================= 1.3 FACTORING =======================
A['1.3']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.11–13 (integer factorization problem, Shor's solution, 3.7 Factoring integers); QAI.pdf p.31–32 (order-finding and factoring, N = 15 example, significance for cryptography)",blocks=[
("def","**Factoring integers** means expressing a composite number N as a product of prime numbers (e.g. 15 = 3 × 5, 91 = 7 × 13). Multiplying primes is easy, but recovering them from a large N is believed to be **very hard classically**; a quantum computer factors efficiently by reducing factoring to **order finding** (Shor's algorithm)."),
("h","1. Importance"),
("b",["**RSA cryptography:** public key contains N = p·q (617 digits for RSA-2048); security rests on the difficulty of factoring N (QAI.pdf p.32).","Number theory, primality and cryptanalysis research."]),
("h","2. Classical methods"),
("t",["Method","Time","Remark"],[["Trial division","O(√N)","Exponential in number of digits"],["Quadratic sieve / GNFS","exp(c·(ln N)^(1/3)(ln ln N)^(2/3))","Best known, still super-polynomial"],["Shor (quantum)","≈ O((log N)³)","Polynomial"]],[1.7,2.9,2.0]),
("h","3. Reduction of factoring to order finding"),
("p","If r is the order of a modulo N (aʳ ≡ 1) and r is even, then aʳ − 1 = (a^(r/2) − 1)(a^(r/2) + 1) ≡ 0 (mod N). N divides the product, but (if a^(r/2) ≢ ±1) divides neither factor, so **gcd(a^(r/2) ± 1, N)** give non-trivial factors."),
("h","4. Algorithm steps"),
("n",["If N is even → factor 2. If N = bᵏ (prime power) → classical check.","Pick random a, 1 < a < N. If gcd(a, N) > 1 → this gcd is a factor.","**Quantum order finding:** find r with aʳ ≡ 1 (mod N) (superposition + modular exponentiation + inverse QFT + continued fractions).","If r is odd or a^(r/2) ≡ −1 (mod N) → go back to step 2 (happens with probability ≤ ½).","Output gcd(a^(r/2) − 1, N) and gcd(a^(r/2) + 1, N)."]),
("d",dg('s[label="Composite N",shape=oval]; a[label="Choose a, 1<a<N\\ngcd(a,N) = 1 ?"]; f0[label="gcd is a factor",shape=oval]; q[label="Quantum order finding\\nr : a^r ≡ 1 (mod N)",fillcolor="#E2EFDA"]; c[label="r even and\\na^(r/2) ≢ −1 ?",shape=diamond,fillcolor="#FFF2CC"]; g[label="p = gcd(a^(r/2)−1, N)\\nq = gcd(a^(r/2)+1, N)"]; e[label="N = p × q",shape=oval]; s->a; a->f0[label="no"]; a->q[label="yes"]; q->c; c->g[label="yes"]; c->a[label="no: new a"]; g->e;',"TB"),"Flowchart for factoring N via quantum order finding",3.7),
("p","**Blocks:** the gcd test handles lucky choices; the green block is the only quantum step; the diamond checks the period is useful; the final gcds give the factors."),
("h","5. Example 1 (successful): N = 21, a = 2"),
("b",["2ˣ mod 21: 2, 4, 8, 16, 11, 1 → **r = 6** (even).","a^(r/2) = 2³ = 8; 8 ≢ −1 (= 20) mod 21 ✔.","gcd(8 − 1, 21) = gcd(7, 21) = **7**;  gcd(8 + 1, 21) = gcd(9, 21) = **3** → **21 = 3 × 7**."]),
("h","6. Example 2 (failure case): N = 15, a = 14"),
("b",["14² = 196 ≡ 1 (mod 15) → r = 2, but a^(r/2) = 14 ≡ −1 (mod 15) → gcds are trivial (15 and 1).","So choose another a (e.g. a = 7 gives r = 4 → factors 3 and 5)."]),
("h","7. Significance"),
("b",["Efficient quantum factoring breaks RSA → need for **post-quantum cryptography** (lattice-, code-, hash-based) – QAI-ii.pdf p.13."]),
("concl","Factoring is reduced to finding the period of aˣ mod N; the quantum computer finds the period quickly and the classical gcd step turns it into the prime factors."),
("tip","**“Factoring = Period + gcd.”** Always show (i) the formula gcd(a^(r/2) ± 1, N), (ii) the two conditions, (iii) a numeric example."),
])
# ======================= 1.4 DEUTSCH =======================
A['1.4']=dict(src="QAI-ii.pdf p.5–8 (3.3 Deutsch algorithm: Qiskit steps, circuit, oracle, Hadamards, result on simulator and IBM hardware), p.17 (question bank items 7–9)",blocks=[
("def","**Deutsch's algorithm** (David Deutsch, 1985) is the first and simplest quantum algorithm. Given a black-box (oracle) function **f : {0,1} → {0,1}**, it decides whether f is **constant** (f(0) = f(1)) or **balanced** (f(0) ≠ f(1)) with **one** query, whereas any classical algorithm needs **two**."),
("h","1. The problem – four possible functions"),
("t",["Function","f(0)","f(1)","Type","Oracle circuit","Measured q₀"],[["f₁","0","0","Constant","nothing","0"],["f₂","1","1","Constant","X on q₁","0"],["f₃ (identity)","0","1","Balanced","CNOT q₀→q₁","1"],["f₄ (NOT)","1","0","Balanced","CNOT + X","1"]],[1.1,0.6,0.6,1.0,1.6,1.0]),
("h","2. Classical analysis"),
("b",["We must compute f(0) **and** f(1) and compare → **2 queries** are unavoidable; one query tells nothing about the global property."]),
("h","3. Quantum circuit"),
("c",["q₀: |0⟩","q₁: |1⟩"],[("H",0,"H",0),("H",1,"H",0),("U2",0,1,"U_f",1),("H",0,"H",2),("M",0,3)],"Deutsch circuit: U_f|x⟩|y⟩ = |x⟩|y ⊕ f(x)⟩",["0 ⇒ constant\n1 ⇒ balanced",""],4.6),
("p","**Components:** q₀ = input qubit; q₁ = helper (output) qubit, prepared in |1⟩ (book: X gate on q₁); first Hadamards = superposition; U_f = oracle; last Hadamard = interference; meter = read q₀."),
("h","4. Step-by-step analysis"),
("n",["Initial state: |ψ₀⟩ = |0⟩|1⟩.","After H ⊗ H: |ψ₁⟩ = ½ (|0⟩ + |1⟩)(|0⟩ − |1⟩) – both inputs present; helper is |−⟩.",
"Oracle: U_f|x⟩|−⟩ = (−1)^f(x)|x⟩|−⟩ (**phase kick-back**) ⇒ |ψ₂⟩ = (1/√2)[(−1)^f(0)|0⟩ + (−1)^f(1)|1⟩] ⊗ |−⟩.",
"If f is **constant**: q₀ = ±(|0⟩ + |1⟩)/√2 = ±|+⟩.  If f is **balanced**: q₀ = ±(|0⟩ − |1⟩)/√2 = ±|−⟩.",
"Final H on q₀: H|+⟩ = |0⟩, H|−⟩ = |1⟩ ⇒ |ψ₃⟩ = ±|f(0) ⊕ f(1)⟩ ⊗ |−⟩.","**Measure q₀:** 0 ⇒ constant, 1 ⇒ balanced – with certainty."]),
("h","5. Worked example: f = identity (balanced)"),
("p","|ψ₂⟩ ∝ (+|0⟩ − |1⟩)|−⟩ because f(0)=0 gives +, f(1)=1 gives −. Then H(|0⟩ − |1⟩)/√2 = |1⟩ → measurement 1 → **balanced**. In the book's Qiskit run the simulator returns 1 with probability 1.000; the IBM 16-qubit device returns 1 with ≈ 0.937 (the rest is hardware **noise**) – QAI-ii.pdf p.8."),
("h","6. Formulas"),
("f",["U_f |x⟩|y⟩ = |x⟩|y ⊕ f(x)⟩ ;  U_f |x⟩|−⟩ = (−1)^f(x) |x⟩|−⟩","Result = f(0) ⊕ f(1)  (0 constant, 1 balanced)"]),
("h","7. Complexity and significance"),
("t",["Aspect","Classical","Quantum"],[["Queries to f","2","**1**"],["Information obtained","f(0) and f(1) separately","Only f(0) ⊕ f(1) (global property)"],["Key principles","–","Superposition, phase kick-back, interference"]],[1.8,2.4,2.4]),
("b",["First proof that a quantum computer can beat a classical one on a well-defined task; foundation for Deutsch–Jozsa, Simon and Shor.","Limitation: toy problem with no direct practical use; speed-up only 2×."]),
("concl","By querying f once on a superposition and using interference, Deutsch's algorithm learns whether f is constant or balanced – a task needing two classical queries."),
("tip","**“H–H, Uf, H, measure: 0 = Constant, 1 = Balanced.”** Prepare the helper in |1⟩ (it becomes |−⟩), otherwise no phase kick-back occurs."),
])
